 ## Background
-Generative NeRF models emerged as an extension of the original NeRF [S1] to address the limitation of reconstructing only single, static scenes. Early works like [StyleNeRF](8659-stylenerf.md) and [HyperNeRFGAN](8765-hypernerfgan.md) adapted GAN architectures (specifically StyleGAN) to the 3D domain, enabling the synthesis of diverse 3D-consistent images [S1]. Subsequent methods like [pi-GAN](10298-pi-gan.md) explored implicit radiance fields with SIREN activations for better high-frequency detail [S2]. More recent approaches like [Latent-NeRF](9110-latent-nerf.md) and [DiffusioNeRF](8703-diffusionerf.md) integrate diffusion models to leverage large-scale 2D priors for text-guided or shape-guided 3D generation [S1, S2]. These methods are widely used in 3D-aware image synthesis, virtual try-on, and content creation [S1, S2].
+Generative NeRF models emerged as an extension of the original NeRF framework [S1]. While standard NeRF reconstructs a single static scene from images, generative variants aim to learn a distribution over 3D shapes and appearances. Early works like [GRAF](9572-generative-and-style-based-models.md) and [pi-GAN](10298-pi-gan.md) adapted GAN architectures to 3D radiance fields. Subsequent methods like [StyleNeRF](8659-stylenerf.md) and [HyperNeRFGAN](8765-hypernerfgan.md) introduced style-based and hypernetwork-based conditioning to improve resolution and controllability. More recently, methods like [Latent-NeRF](9110-latent-nerf.md) have integrated diffusion models to leverage large-scale 2D priors for 3D generation. These models are used in computer graphics for content creation, virtual reality, and 3D editing [S1, S2, S3].
 
